 The preview reads exactly one sector: the first 128 bytes name a file
 better than a two-megabyte read that would sit on a heap with no
 collector.

 We say:

Section: Palette and Geometry

 WORKS-17. The pane's pens are ROLES in the desk's palette rather than seven
 fixed numbers, so the pane follows the colour scheme. The palette arrives as
 a parameter and not by citation: `GopDesk` cites this chapter, so reading
 `dk-pal ds` here would be a cycle (`works-desk-contract.md` section 6).

 The roles are the ones the already-converted panes use, so a pane written
 next has somewhere to copy from: a floating window's body is `pal-muted` as
 the Welcome window's is, its title bar `pal-secondary`, its caption
 `pal-accent`, its body text `pal-fg`, and both its labels and its Esc line
 `pal-primary`, which is what `desk-mon-row` and the shutdown warning already
 do.

 One pen has no role of its own and is said so here rather than left to look
 deliberate: `dim` and `green` collapse into `pal-primary`, because they were
 a grey label and a green hint and the palette has one label role, not two.
 The editor's current-line band stays `pal-border`, which carries no text of
 its own and only has to sit between the window body and the title bar.

 That collapse is why a directory row now takes `bright` and an ordinary file
 `text`, where the fixed ramp had the two greys the other way up. `dim` is
 the LABEL pen, and painting every file with it made the listing green under
 the default scheme, which read as a hint rather than as a filename. The
 emphasis a reader wants is still there and `<DIR>` still says which is
 which.

  gfl-bg : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-bg (p) = p.pal-muted

  gfl-title : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-title (p) = p.pal-secondary

  gfl-text : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-text (p) = p.pal-fg

  gfl-bright : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-bright (p) = p.pal-accent

 A selected row is the scheme's accent with an ink chosen to be legible on
 it, rather than a quiet band with a fixed pen over it. `pal-border` was the
 quiet band and it does not stay quiet: under `lcars` it is orange, and the
 `pal-accent` text over it came out cyan on orange. `theme-ink-on` answers
 with one of this palette's own text colours by the accent's brightness, so
 the pair is legible in every scheme including ones not written yet.

  gfl-sel : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-sel (p) = p.pal-accent

  gfl-on-sel : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-on-sel (p) = theme-ink-on p (p.pal-accent)

  gfl-dim : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-dim (p) = p.pal-primary

  gfl-hint : Palette -> Integer
  gfl-hint (p) = p.pal-primary

 The pane floats over the desktop to the right of the sidebar. Every
 measure derives from the panel rectangle and the scale, so the same
 code lays out 640x480 and 1280x800.

  gfl-px : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-px (w) (s) = 90 * s

  gfl-py : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-py (h) (s) = 30 * s

  gfl-pw : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-pw (w) (s) = w - 100 * s

  gfl-ph : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-ph (h) (s) = h - 74 * s

  gfl-row-h : Integer -> Integer
  gfl-row-h (s) = 18 * s

  gfl-rows-y0 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-rows-y0 (h) (s) = gfl-py h s + 44 * s

  gfl-rows-vis : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-rows-vis (h) (s) = (gfl-ph h s - 74 * s) / gfl-row-h s

  gfl-row-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-row-at (w) (h) (s) (top) (n) (mx) (my) =
    let y0 = gfl-rows-y0 h s
    in if mx < gfl-px w s then -1
    else if mx >= gfl-px w s + gfl-pw w s then -1
    else if my < y0 then -1
    else let row = top + (my - y0) / gfl-row-h s
    in if row >= n then -1
    else if row - top >= gfl-rows-vis h s then -1
    else row

  gfl-close-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gfl-close-hit (w) (h) (s) (mx) (my) =
    let bx = gfl-px w s + gfl-pw w s - 16 * s
    in if mx < bx then False
    else if mx >= gfl-px w s + gfl-pw w s then False
    else if my < gfl-py h s then False
    else if my >= gfl-py h s + 20 * s then False
    else True

  gfl-clamp-top : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-clamp-top (sel) (top) (vis) =
    if sel < top then sel
    else if sel >= top + vis then sel - vis + 1
    else top

Section: Formatting

  gfl-pad : Text, Integer -> Text
  gfl-pad (t) (width) =
    if text-length t >= width then t
    else gfl-pad (t & " ") width

  gfl-is-dir : Fat16DirEntry -> Boolean
  gfl-is-dir (e) = bit-and (e.de-attr) 16 == 16

  gfl-size-text : Fat16DirEntry -> Text
  gfl-size-text (e) = if gfl-is-dir e then "<DIR>" else show (e.de-size)

  gfl-row-text : Fat16DirEntry -> Text
  gfl-row-text (e) = gfl-pad (e.de-name) 16 & gfl-size-text e

  gfl-hex-digit : Integer -> Text
  gfl-hex-digit (v) =
    if v == 0 then "0" else if v == 1 then "1" else if v == 2 then "2"
    else if v == 3 then "3" else if v == 4 then "4" else if v == 5 then "5"
    else if v == 6 then "6" else if v == 7 then "7" else if v == 8 then "8"
    else if v == 9 then "9" else if v == 10 then "a" else if v == 11 then "b"
    else if v == 12 then "c" else if v == 13 then "d" else if v == 14 then "e"
    else "f"

  gfl-hex-byte : Integer -> Text
  gfl-hex-byte (b) = gfl-hex-digit (bit-and (bit-shru b 4) 15) & gfl-hex-digit (bit-and b 15)

  gfl-ascii : Integer -> Text
  gfl-ascii (c) =
    if c < 32 then "."
    else if c > 126 then "."
    else char-to-text (code-to-char (from-unicode c))

 A byte costs four character cells on the line -- two hex digits and a
 space in the run, one more in the ASCII column -- plus one cell for the
 single space between the two halves. Sixteen bytes is therefore 65 cells,
 which at an 8-pixel advance is 1040 pixels and wider than the panel was:
 the ASCII column ran out past the right frame and painted on the desktop
 behind it. The count is derived from the panel rather than fixed so a
 narrower mode drops to twelve or eight instead of overflowing.

  gfl-hex-n : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-hex-n (w) (s) =
    let cells = (gfl-pw w s - 20 * s) / (8 * s)
    in let fit = (cells - 1) / 4
    in if fit > 16 then 16 else if fit < 1 then 1 else fit

  gfl-hex-line : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  gfl-hex-line (buf) (off) (n) =
    gfl-hex-run buf off n "" & " " & gfl-ascii-run buf off n ""

  gfl-hex-run : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  gfl-hex-run (buf) (off) (n) (acc) =
    if n == 0 then acc
    else gfl-hex-run buf (off + 1) (n - 1) (acc & gfl-hex-byte (peek-byte buf off) & " ")

  gfl-ascii-run : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  gfl-ascii-run (buf) (off) (n) (acc) =
    if n == 0 then acc
    else gfl-ascii-run buf (off + 1) (n - 1) (acc & gfl-ascii (peek-byte buf off))

  gfl-path-text : List Text -> Text
  gfl-path-text (names) = gfl-path-loop names 0 "ESP:/"

  gfl-path-loop : List Text, Integer, Text -> Text
  gfl-path-loop (names) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length names then acc
    else gfl-path-loop names (i + 1) (acc & list-at names i & "/")

Section: Drawing

  gfl-draw-frame : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Palette, Text -> Integer
  gfl-draw-frame (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (pal) (title) =
    let px = gfl-px w s
    in let py = gfl-py h s
    in let pw = gfl-pw w s
    in let ph = gfl-ph h s
    in let body = gop-fill-rect base stride px py pw ph (gfl-bg pal)
    in let bar = gop-fill-rect base stride px py pw (20 * s) (gfl-title pal)
    in let cap = gop-draw-text base stride font (px + 6 * s) (py + 2 * s) title (gfl-bright pal) s 0
    in gop-draw-text base stride font (px + pw - 14 * s) (py + 2 * s) "x" (gfl-bright pal) s 0

  gfl-draw-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Palette, IconKit, List Fat16DirEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-draw-rows (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (pal) (kit) (entries) (sel) (top) (i) =
    let n = list-length entries
    in if top + i >= n then 0
    else if i >= gfl-rows-vis h s then 0
    else let idx = top + i
    in let y = gfl-rows-y0 h s + i * gfl-row-h s
    in let px = gfl-px w s
    in let hl = if idx == sel then gop-fill-rect base stride px y (gfl-pw w s) (gfl-row-h s) (gfl-sel pal) else 0
    in let e = list-at entries idx
    in let fg = if idx == sel then gfl-on-sel pal else if gfl-is-dir e then gfl-bright pal else gfl-text pal
    in let ico = gicon-pick kit (gfl-kind e)
    in let d = gicon-blit base stride ico (px + 10 * s) (y + s) (gfl-icon-scale s) fg
    in let t = gop-draw-text base stride font (px + 10 * s + gfl-icon-col kit s) (y + s) (gfl-row-text e) fg s 0
    in gfl-draw-rows base w h stride s font pal kit entries sel top (i + 1)

  gfl-draw-list : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Palette, List Fat16DirEntry, Integer, Integer, List Text -> Integer
  gfl-draw-list (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (pal) (entries) (sel) (top) (names) =
    let f = gfl-draw-frame base w h stride s font pal ("Files  " & gfl-path-text names)
    in let px = gfl-px w s
    in let py = gfl-py h s
    in let kit = gicon-kit
    in let hdr = gop-draw-text base stride font (px + 10 * s + gfl-icon-col kit s) (py + 26 * s) (gfl-pad "name" 16 & "size") (gfl-dim pal) s 0
    in let rows = gfl-draw-rows base w h stride s font pal kit entries sel top 0
    in let none = if list-length entries == 0 then gop-draw-text base stride font (px + 10 * s) (gfl-rows-y0 h s) "(empty directory)" (gfl-dim pal) s 0 else 0
    in gop-draw-text base stride font (px + 10 * s) (py + gfl-ph h s - 18 * s) "Up/Down move   Enter open   Esc back" (gfl-hint pal) s 0

  gfl-is-source : Fat16DirEntry -> Boolean
  gfl-is-source (e) = gfl-ext-is (e.de-name) "SRC"

  gfl-ext-is : Text, Text -> Boolean
  gfl-ext-is (name) (ext) =
    let n = text-length name
    in let m = text-length ext
    in if n < m then False
    else substring name (n - m) m == ext

 `gfl-is-source` decides whether the PREVIEW runs the highlighter and is
 left as it was. What the type column distinguishes is a wider question
 than what the highlighter can parse, so the two tests are separate: an
 `INDEX.TXT` reads as text in the listing and still previews as hex.

 A short name on the medium is upper case and trimmed to `NAME.EXT`
 (`fat16-extract-name`), which is what these compare against.

  gfl-is-image : Fat16DirEntry -> Boolean
  gfl-is-image (e) = gfl-ext-is (e.de-name) "BMP" | gfl-ext-is (e.de-name) "PNG" | gfl-ext-is (e.de-name) "JPG"

  gfl-is-textish : Fat16DirEntry -> Boolean
  gfl-is-textish (e) = gfl-is-source e | gfl-ext-is (e.de-name) "COD" | gfl-ext-is (e.de-name) "TXT" | gfl-ext-is (e.de-name) "MD"

  gfl-kind : Fat16DirEntry -> Integer
  gfl-kind (e) =
    if gfl-is-dir e then gicon-dir
    else if gfl-is-textish e then gicon-src
    else if gfl-is-image e then gicon-img
    else gicon-bin

 The icon is drawn at twice the panel scale so an 8x8 mask stands 16 rows
 tall, which is the height `gop-draw-text` gives a glyph at the same
 scale. Both start at the same y and the row needs no centring arithmetic.

 The column the icon costs is measured off the artwork rather than written
 down twice, so the header and the rows cannot drift apart.

  gfl-icon-scale : Integer -> Integer
  gfl-icon-scale (s) = 2 * s

  gfl-icon-col : IconKit, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-icon-col (kit) (s) = gicon-size (gicon-pick kit gicon-bin) (gfl-icon-scale s) + 4 * s

  gfl-draw-preview : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Palette, Fat16DirEntry, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-draw-preview (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (pal) (e) (buf) (got) =
    let f = gfl-draw-frame base w h stride s font pal ("File  " & e.de-name)
    in let px = gfl-px w s
    in let py = gfl-py h s
    in let sz = gop-draw-text base stride font (px + 10 * s) (py + 26 * s) (show (e.de-size) & " bytes") (gfl-bright pal) s 0
    in let body = if gfl-is-source e
      then gfl-source-lines base w h stride s font buf 0 got 0
      else gfl-preview-lines base w h stride s font pal buf 0 (if got > 128 then 128 else got)
    in gop-draw-text base stride font (px + 10 * s) (py + gfl-ph h s - 18 * s) "Esc back" (gfl-hint pal) s 0

Section: Source Preview

 The sector's bytes are ASCII on the medium -- the image writer converts
 each CCE code point out through `to-unicode` on the way in -- so each
 byte comes back through `from-unicode` to become a Codex character
 again. Reading them as CCE directly is the boundary bug this project
 has shipped more than once, and here it would render every letter as
 the wrong glyph.

  gfl-lf : Integer
  gfl-lf = 10

  gfl-src-line-end : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-src-line-end (buf) (off) (n) =
    if off >= n then off
    else if peek-byte buf off == gfl-lf then off
    else gfl-src-line-end buf (off + 1) n

  gfl-src-text : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  gfl-src-text (buf) (off) (stop) (acc) =
    if off >= stop then acc
    else let b = peek-byte buf off
    in let ch = if b < 32 then " " else char-to-text (code-to-char (from-unicode b))
    in gfl-src-text buf (off + 1) stop (acc & ch)

  gfl-source-lines : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-source-lines (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (buf) (off) (n) (row) =
    if off >= n then 0
    else if row >= gfl-rows-vis h s then 0
    else let stop = gfl-src-line-end buf off n
    in let line = gfl-src-text buf off stop ""
    in let y = gfl-rows-y0 h s + row * gfl-row-h s
    in let d = gfl-draw-runs base w h stride s font line (syn-line line) 0 y
    in gfl-source-lines base w h stride s font buf (stop + 1) n (row + 1)

  gfl-draw-runs : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Text, List SynRun, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-draw-runs (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (line) (runs) (i) (y) =
    if i >= list-length runs then 0
    else let r = list-at runs i
    in let t = substring line (r.sr-start) (r.sr-len)
    in let x = gfl-px w s + 10 * s + (r.sr-start) * 8 * s
    in let d = gop-draw-text base stride font x y t (syn-colour (r.sr-class)) s 0
    in gfl-draw-runs base w h stride s font line runs (i + 1) y

  gfl-preview-lines : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Palette, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfl-preview-lines (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (pal) (buf) (off) (n) =
    if off >= n then 0
    else let per = gfl-hex-n w s
    in let take = if n - off > per then per else n - off
    in let y = gfl-rows-y0 h s + (off / per) * gfl-row-h s
    in let t = gop-draw-text base stride font (gfl-px w s + 10 * s) y (gfl-hex-line buf off take) (gfl-text pal) s 0
    in gfl-preview-lines base w h stride s font pal buf (off + per) n

Section: Navigation State

  gfl-pop-int : List Integer -> List Integer
  gfl-pop-int (xs) = gfl-pop-int-loop xs 0 []

  gfl-pop-int-loop : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gfl-pop-int-loop (xs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length xs - 1 then acc
    else gfl-pop-int-loop xs (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

  gfl-pop-text : List Text -> List Text
  gfl-pop-text (xs) = gfl-pop-text-loop xs 0 []

  gfl-pop-text-loop : List Text, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  gfl-pop-text-loop (xs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length xs - 1 then acc
    else gfl-pop-text-loop xs (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

  gfl-cur-dir : List Integer -> Integer
  gfl-cur-dir (clusters) = list-at clusters (list-length clusters - 1)

  gfl-list-cur : Fat16Volume, List Integer -> [Device.Port] List Fat16DirEntry
  gfl-list-cur (vol) (clusters) = act
    let cur = gfl-cur-dir clusters
    in if cur < 0 then gfat-list-root vol
    else gfat-list-dir vol cur
  end

Section: Preview

 The preview was a modal loop until 2026-08-18 and is a paint now. It never
 repaints: nothing scrolls it, and the only band the desk redraws every second
 is the taskbar, which no Files screen reaches. So the caller paints once, sets
 its mode, and answers ordinary events after that -- there is nothing to keep,
 which is why this pane needed no state for it beyond one integer.

  gfl-preview-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Palette, Integer, Fat16Volume, Fat16DirEntry -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfl-preview-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (s) (font) (pal) (cs) (vol) (e) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    if e.de-size == 0 | e.de-cluster < 2 then act
      f <- gfl-draw-frame base w h stride s font pal ("File  " & e.de-name)
      t <- gop-draw-text base stride font (gfl-px w s + 10 * s) (gfl-py h s + 26 * s) "empty file" (gfl-dim pal) s 0
      gop-draw-text base stride font (gfl-px w s + 10 * s) (gfl-py h s + gfl-ph h s - 18 * s) "Esc back" (gfl-hint pal) s 0
    end
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector (fat16-cluster-sector vol (e.de-cluster))
      let got = if b.dr-ok then (if e.de-size < 512 then e.de-size else 512) else 0
      in gfl-draw-preview base w h stride s font pal e (b.dr-buf) got
    end
  end
Section: Loop

 The loop moved to `GopDesk` on 2026-08-18. Files is a step pane now: the
 desk owns the poll and calls `desk-files-step` with one event, so the
 taskbar clock keeps running while this pane is up. What stayed here is
 every measure, every pixel and the modal preview; what left is
 `gfl-run`, `gfl-loop`, `gfl-activate`, `gfl-back`, `gfl-move` and
 `gfl-redraw`, whose state is now `FilesState` in `GopDesk`. The contract
 is `apps/works/works-desk-contract.md` section 0.